    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-rhantos-l242-2/",
      "html": "<p>With almost 150 accepted species, the genus Hypostomus (in the broadest sense) is the most species-rich group of loricariid catfish. Unfortunately, there is no current revision of all species and so the determination of Hypostomus is usually a laborious affair, in the end often only a questionable result.</p><p>Hypostomus rhantos is pleasingly easy to recognize. The L-number 242 was applied on it before its scientific description. The fine dot pattern and the orange tending coloration make the species almost unmistakable; only Hypostomus micromaculatus from Surinam has similarly small dots.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-rhantos-l242-2/",
      "html": "<p>Hypostomus rhantos comes from the upper Orinoco in Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypostomus-rhantos-l242-2/",
      "html": "<p>Hypostomus rhantos is quite attractive in color and certainly one of the most beautiful Hypostomus species. It reaches about 20 cm in length and is a typical Aufwuchs eater. As by-catch some Hypostomus sp. L192 and a Hypostomus species from the Cochliodon group were received, which is almost identical in color to L192, but has a completely different dentition; it is probably Hypostomus (Cochliodon) sculpodon or an undescribed species.</p>"
    }
  ],
